WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.160
As with most major Marvel movies, fan speculation
surrounding the latest upcoming Spider-Man

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.800
sequel has been running rampant, with both
plausible and downright bizarre theories and

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.300
predictions.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.160
Here are some of the craziest fan theories
we've found that try and predict the events

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.760
in Spider-Man: Far from Home.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.380
Homeland's Numan Acar plays the cryptic role
of "Dmitri" in Far from Home.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.400
While the specific details of Acar's role
are still being kept under wraps, fans of

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.910
Marvel Comics were quick to point out that
his character shares a name with a notorious

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.480
Spider-Man foe: Dmitri Smerdyakov, a.k.a.
the Chameleon.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.120
In a recent interview with Comicbook.com,
director Jon Watts was asked point-blank whether

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.140
or not Acar's character was in fact the Chameleon,
and his answer was purposefully unclear.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.300
"Well, Dmitri in the film works for Nick Fury
and I think anyone who works for Nick Fury,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.020
they certainly have a mysterious past…

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.480
We're not specifically saying that he's…
but we're not not saying."

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.760
Given that Spider-Man: Homecoming included
the Vulture, the Tinkerer, the Prowler, two

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.839
Shockers, and even a brief glimpse of Scorpion,
it's not hard to imagine that Far from Home

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.120
will also feature appearances from multiple
villains from Spidey's notorious rogues' gallery.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.040
If Dmitri does in fact turn out to be the
Chameleon, it will add an engaging element

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.800
of mystery to the sequel, leaving audiences
guessing who's really who.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.800
Speaking of which...

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.320
Could the Nick Fury shown in the Far from
Home trailers actually be the Chameleon in

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:16.820
disguise?

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.720
This theory seemingly stems from the fact
that, in the comics, the Chameleon started

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.180
out as a Soviet spy with unique abilities.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.110
Given that Far from Home takes place in Europe,
Chameleon's foreign origin could match up

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:27.880
quite nicely.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.480
Nerdist takes this theory one step further,
stating that Fury's wardrobe in the Far from

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.280
Home trailers seems a little suspect.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.460
In the footage, Fury dons the same long black
trench coat he wore way back in the early

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.280
phases of the MCU like in the first Avengers
film before SHIELD was deconstructed.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.480
This is a stark contrast to Fury's apparel
in post-SHIELD appearances, in which he has

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.980
worn more discreet outfits.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.080
This fashion faux pas could be proof that
this isn't the real Nick Fury, who presumably

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.380
threw all his cool trench coats into the trash.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.480
This potential shocker could be the "major
twist" that Tom Holland teased when ScreenRant

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.920
visited the Far from Home set last year when
he said:

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.800
"There's a scene in this film where audiences
will feel like they were punched in the face.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.950
Even filming it, I remember walking out and
then watching it again on the monitors and

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.239
asking Jon [Watts], 'Are you sure that's okay?'

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:13.099
And he's like, 'No, it's not.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.440
People are going to hate this scene.'"

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.940
But what if the Chameleon-is-Fury twist isn't
coming.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.500
What are some other swerves Far From Home
might pull on an unsuspecting audience?

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:25.340
One of the wildest fan theories concerning
the new Spidey flick is that we'll learn that

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.660
MJ, Peter's new love interest, is actually
the daughter of Nick Fury.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:29.480
Possible?

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:30.060
Sure!

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:30.720
Plausible?

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:31.460
Eh!

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.620
We do know that Fury mentioned having had
a wife in Captain America: The Winter Soldier:

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.140
"My wife kicked me out."

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.320
"I didn't know you were married."

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.020
"There's a lot of things you don't know about
me."

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.840
Of course, in that scene Fury is on the run
from HYDRA, so while he said he's a family

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.900
man, there's no way to know if he's ever even
kissed a girl, let alone had a wife or a daughter.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.080
Overall, this one seems pretty unlikely.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:56.629
Speaking of unlikely...

00:02:56.629 --> 00:03:00.689
In the comics, Ned Leeds is a Daily Bugle
reporter who undergoes a brief stint as the

00:03:00.689 --> 00:03:05.090
supervillain Hobgoblin after being brainwashed
by Roderick Kingsley, the actual man behind

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:05.580
the mask.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.060
Although the MCU's Ned Leeds is more similar
to Ganke Lee, the comics-obsessed best friend

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:14.129
of Miles Morales' Spider-Man, the name alone
is enough for some fans to have predicted

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:15.940
a dark turn for Ned in Far from Home.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.280
Could Ned's switch to supervillainy be the
big twist awaiting moviegoers?

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.040
Well, the real issue with this theory is that
the Green Goblin hasn't yet been introduced

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:23.820
to the MCU.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.599
And without first introducing that villain,
how would introducing the dollar store version

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:28.520
make any sense?

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.140
Then again, while we're on the subject of
the Green Goblin...

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.120
In Spider-Man: Homecoming,we learned that
Tony Stark had sold Avengers Tower and was

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.920
in the process of completely moving all of
the team's various super-props to their upstate

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:40.520
facility.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.120
Unsurprisingly, a sale of that magnitude made
fans perk up their ears, prompting discussions

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.700
about the potential purchaser of the New York
high rise.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.420
Additionally, in the trailer for Far from
Home, eagle-eyed viewers noticed that Spidey

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.600
swings past what appears to be the old Stark
Tower, only now it's under construction.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.700
So, who could the new owner or owners be?

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.980
In the comic books, Norman Osborn briefly
took over the Avengers, recruiting suspect

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:06.849
characters to the team, including Moonstone,
Bullseye, and Venom, while donning the Iron

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:07.820
Patriot armor.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:11.700
The team, better known as the Dark Avengers,
took up residence in Avengers Tower.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.870
In what would be a crazy twist, fans of the
MCU have been theorizing that the so-far non-existent

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:21.330
MCU version of Norman Osborn is the buyer
alluded to in Spider-Man: Homecoming, making

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.560
him the future owner of Avengers Tower.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.810
One of the more popular theories gaining traction
online is that Osborn's reveal will take place

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.000
in one of Far from Home's two end-credits
scenes.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:34.020
The theory, originally posited by a redditor
who claims to be a special effects artist

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.520
for Disney, posits that not only will the
scene reveal that Osborn is in fact the new

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.180
owner of Avengers Tower, now proudly showcasing
"OSCORP" where the "A" once was, but also

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.479
confirm the rise of the Sinister Six, a team
consisting of Mysterio, Chameleon, Shocker,

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.080
Vulture, Scorpion, and Osborn's own alter
ego the Green Goblin.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.880
This would certainly get fans excited for
the third solo Spider-Man film, which Tom

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.360
Holland previously confirmed back in 2017.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:01.420
Although this would certainly be a satisfying
reveal, there's more than one famous tower

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.420
in Marvel Comics' version of New York City.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:08.540
With Disney's multi-billion dollar acquisition
of 21st Century Fox finally completed, the

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.460
other logical prediction is that Avengers
Tower could soon become the Baxter Building,

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.270
home to the Fantastic Four, whose movie rights
were also returned to Disney/Marvel in the

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:16.760
deal.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:21.360
If this theory is indeed true, it would pave
the way for the likes of Mr. Fantastic, Invisible

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.340
Woman, the Thing, and the Human Torch to finally
make their long-awaited return to the MCU

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.360
and suit up alongside the remaining heroes.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:33.400
One of the most exciting fan theories about
Far from Home is that the X-Men will somehow

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.540
be introduced to the MCU via a parallel universe.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:41.460
In the Far from Home trailer, Nick Fury explains
to Peter Parker that Mysterio is from another

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.220
dimension, inadvertently brought to their
Earth as a result of Iron Man's life-sacrificing

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:46.800
snap in Avengers: Endgame.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.960
"Beck is from Earth, just not ours.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.540
The snap tore a hole in our dimension."

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.580
"You're saying there's a multiverse?"

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.280
If Mysterio is indeed telling the truth about
his origin, he's living proof that there is

00:05:58.280 --> 00:05:59.820
a multiverse in the MCU.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:04.080
Parallel universes and inter-dimensional travel
would be some smart ways for Marvel Studios

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.639
to introduce their new toys to their already
overflowing sandbox and it worked in Spider-Man:

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.500
Into the Spider-Verse.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.340
That said, we're talking about Mysterio here
a supervillainous master of manipulation and

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:14.940
deception.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.840
If internet sleuths really want to theorize
his presence here is somehow proof that the

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.530
X-Men are coming through the dimensional divide
as well, they should probably crack a comic

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:23.160
book or two.

